  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Strong as a minimal, evolvable container with explicit dispatch boundaries, but weak on this scenario because per-hop authorization failure behavior is not defined by the envelope itself; it can only be expressed by additional wrapper or payload protocols.",
    "strengths": [
      "Explicit pCID dispatch and no speculative parsing keep the envelope/handler boundary clear.",
      "Unknown-pCID opaque handling supports sparse knowledge and long-lived migration.",
      "Hop-local evidence can be layered as wrapper envelopes without changing the base envelope shape."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The specimen does not define ring membership, forwarding authorization, or a canonical refusal record.",
      "It does not choose whether auth failure breaks the ring, skips a hop, or triggers reconfiguration.",
      "Human and LLM auditability depend on wrapper-specific handler availability."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Authorization semantics may splinter across many wrapper protocols and harm interop.",
      "Opaque wrappers can preserve bytes while still leaving later auditors unable to explain a refusal.",
      "Mixed protocol versions may encode hop-failure outcomes incompatibly across long-lived rings."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What canonical wrapper records Bob's refusal so Carol and auditors can observe it locally?",
      "When Bob cannot forward, what is the required outcome: ring break, hop skip, or membership reconfiguration?",
      "How is forwarding authority bound to ring membership epoch and exact envelope bytes across key and protocol rotation?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
